\openmidiport opens a MIDI output port.
It takes one argument, the number of the port, as printed
by \listmidiports:

\listmidiports
\openmidiport 1

\listmidiports must be called first, otherwise you will
get a "MIDI port doesn't exist" error, as the list of
ports is read by that command.
A port can be opened only once with this command. Opening
it again gives an error.

Once open, the port can be used by \ctlchange and
\pgmchange, and it gets the MIDI clock (see \midiclock).
Instruments open their port with their midiport method, so
you don't need this command for them:

\inst1.midiport 1

A port that an instrument has opened can also be opened
with this command, so that it gets the MIDI clock too.
